WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.670 --> 00:00:03.690
 There's a moment
when you're just a fan,

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:07.090
and there's a moment
when you're a filmmaker.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:11.520
And the one moment can follow
the other by about one minute.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.100
I've been directing films
for almost four decades,

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.090
and I've picked up a few
things along the way.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.340
For the first time ever,
I'll share some of the things

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:22.140
that I haven't shared before.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.335
Really, every filmmaker
starts as a fan.

00:00:25.335 --> 00:00:27.710
I get my ideas the same place
everybody gets their ideas,

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:30.010
out of your imagination.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:36.550
I can't emphasize enough
the importance of curiosity.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:40.840
Every film you've ever watched,
every book you've ever read,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:43.150
every dream you've ever had--

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:47.380
before you can radiate,
you need to absorb.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.435
It's the creativity
law of thermodynamics.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.610
In this MasterClass, I'm going
to talk about storytelling,

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:00.000
suspense, action,
visual effects, design,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.080
all the things you're
going to need to know.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.080
So I'm going to show
a scene right now,

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.330
and then I'll go back
and break it down.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.890
The thing that's important to
remember about "The Terminator"

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.140
is that it's a love story.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.030
Their eyes meet
across a crowded room.

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.920
It's a love story with shotguns.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.170
My goal with "Avatar"
was to have people dream

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.710
with their eyes wide open.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.440
I feel like I'm there.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.050
It looks real.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.090
But it has to be a dream.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.460
When I started out, I was
working as a truck driver.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:28.027
Didn't have much spare cash.

00:01:28.027 --> 00:01:29.610
Certainly couldn't
afford film school.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.020
But all you have to
do is pick up a camera

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.630
and start shooting something
with the intention of making

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.890
a film, and you're a filmmaker.

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.220
And I think it's the
duty of filmmakers

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.920
that have been down the a road
ways to turn around and say,

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:43.780
"all right, there's
a landmine over here,

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.840
there's a landmine over
here, don't step on it."

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.320
So why not share that?

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.640
I'm James Cameron, and
this is my MasterClass.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.790
[MUSIC PLAYING]

